{"qid":"Q1147897","names":["Wey"],"title":"Wey","endonym":null,"capsule":"Wey (衛) was one of the Zhou vassal states, situated in northeastern Henan east of Jin and west of Cao, with an early center at Zhaoge (the former Shang capital); it was founded in the early Western Zhou by Kang Shu, a younger brother of King Wu of Zhou, after the suppression of the Rebellion of the Three Guards. Under Duke Wu (r. c. 812–758 BCE), who reigned some 55 years, Wey reached its political apex and became a center of poetry — the \"Airs of Wey\" in the Classic of Poetry are associated with this region. In 660 BCE invading Rong peoples devastated the state and nearly extinguished it, and Duke Huan of Qi intervened to restore it at a new site. During the Warring States period Wey was reduced to little more than the city of Puyang, its rulers demoted from duke to lord (jun); the last ruler, Lord Jiao, was deposed by Qin Er Shi in 209 BCE, making Wey the final Zhou vassal state to be extinguished.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The political chronology of Wey is documented in the Shiji and Zuozhuan, but its territorial extent — especially during the Warring States period when it was reduced to a rump — is poorly mapped; the from/to attestation window of –750 to –247 likely reflects confident boundary evidence rather than the full span of the state.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Wey (state)","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Duke Wu of Wey","nativeName":"衛武公","roman":"Wey Wu Gong","start":-812,"end":-758,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Duke Zhuang 